  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Patchy ice on elevated surfaces and difficult, but improving, \n   pass travel continues this morning.\n\n-  Periodic light snow and valley rain/snow mix Thursday and \n   Friday.\n\n-  Accumulating snow targets Northwest Montana Friday night into \n   Saturday.\n\nThe primary precipitation band has shifted east of the \nContinental Divide this morning. Abundant low-level moisture \nremains trapped in the valleys, supporting the potential \ndevelopment of fog, freezing fog, and low stratus. As \ntemperatures drop into the 20s and lower 30s, residual moisture \non elevated roadways, bridges, and overpasses will freeze. Areas \nthat received snowfall will also experience enhanced radiational \ncooling, leading to more widespread slick surfaces. Difficult \nwinter driving conditions will continue across mountain passes, \nthough improving steadily especially after daybreak, particularly \ntoward Homestake Pass.\n\nWesterly flow aloft will transition to southwesterly later today \nahead of a Pacific trough dropping into the Great Basin. This \nsystem will lift northeastward, reintroducing shower activity \nacross the Northern Rockies by Thursday morning. The resident \nairmass will remain colder than the previous system, with snow \nlevels holding near 2,000 feet along and north of I-90, and \nbetween 3,000 to 3,500 feet south of I-90. Precipitation will fall\npredominantly as snow, with a rain/snow mix limited to the lowest\nriver valleys. Showers will linger into Friday under a similar \nthermal and moisture profile, keeping precipitation amounts \ngenerally light.\n\nA more impactful system will drop southward out of Canada Friday \nnight into Saturday. The parent low will track east of the \nContinental Divide, forcing a backdoor cold front across the \nregion. North-northeasterly winds will reinvigorate snow showers \nalong and behind this boundary, bringing a classic setup for \naccumulating snow across Northwest Montana and areas along and \nnorth of the I-90 corridor. The Glacier Park and Flathead regions \nare the primary targets, where a broad few inches of accumulation \nare expected, with localized totals up to 6 inches possible under \nterrain and stronger frontogenetic enhancement.\n\nThe active progressive pattern will continue without a significant\nbreak in precipitation nor warmer temperatures. Another low \npressure system moving onshore over the Pacific Northwest will \nmaintain snow and snow shower chances across the region through \nSunday.",
